window.SPL_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"Build AI workflows in minutes, not hours","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html","text":"Turn Markdown into runnable AI workflows: natural-language steps alongside native JavaScript, checked model output, guards and human approval.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Generate reusable functions","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#generate-reusable-functions","text":"@CODEGEN asks the model for JavaScript functions and defines them in the shared scope. Later fences and blockquotes call them like any other function. In code, await codegen(description) does the same and returns the function. With --trace, the generated code is also saved beside the workflow, here as codegen.sieve.js. Its first line hashes the description, and later traced runs reuse a matching file without calling the model: See Code generation for validation and cache rules.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Types the model can read","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#types-the-model-can-read","text":"Types are ordinary JavaScript values built with library functions. They validate data, turn JSON into JavaScript objects and render the JSON Schema that is sent to the model, including any annotations you attach. union(float, int) accepts either number. .methods({...}) makes parsed answers instances with methods, so person.toString() works on the model's output. See Types.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Why SPL?","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#why-spl","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"How a run works","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#how-a-run-works","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Non-goals","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#non-goals","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Where next","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#where-next","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Getting started","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html","text":"Install the CLI, configure OpenAI, Anthropic or Google credentials, then write and refine a workflow in a short edit-run loop.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Install","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#install","text":"Use Node.js >=24.15.0. Native BetterTUI packages support Windows, macOS and Linux on x64/arm64. If native rendering cannot start, the CLI diagnoses the problem and keeps complete plain logs. spl login \u003cprovider> prompts for an API key without echoing it and saves a separate key for each provider in ~/.spl/credentials.json (override the folder with SPL_HOME). It also selects that provider as the default. If the provider's environment key is already set, login selects the provider without prompting or copying the environment secret into the file. Keys are configured locally; login does not make a billable request or verify account access. Alternatively, set OPENAI_API_KEY, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY. Google also accepts GOOGLE_API_KEY and GEMINI_API_KEY, in that order after the canonical name. Environment keys take precedence over saved keys. A single configured provider is detected automatically. With multiple providers, select one with --provider, SPL_PROVIDER, a model such as --model google/gemini-2.5-flash, or a saved login default. See Models and authentication for precedence and defaults. Workflows without model calls, --check and replay need no credentials.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Write a workflow","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#write-a-workflow","text":"The fence is JavaScript and runs first. The blockquote is sent to the model as an agent command; @name refers to the variable the fence declared, and @success asks the agent to end the run with a result, which the CLI prints on stdout.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Document structure","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#document-structure","text":"Fences and blockquotes run in document order in one shared scope. success(value) or an uncaught failure ends the whole document.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"JavaScript in five rules","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#javascript-in-five-rules","text":"Workflow code is ordinary JavaScript. These rules cover what is specific to workflows; Workflow code explains each one.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"The iteration loop","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#the-iteration-loop","text":"Replay needs the same model requests in the same order. After changing a prompt, record a new trace. Recorded model calls, tools and human answers are not repeated during replay; your own JavaScript runs again. See traces and replay.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Use the library without Markdown","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#use-the-library-without-markdown","text":"The same functions are a normal Node.js package. Install spl-lang in a project and import what you need; see the library in Node.js programs.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"VS Code extension","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#vs-code-extension","text":"Install from Marketplace: SPL The extension in vscode/ turns workflow Markdown into a JavaScript editing experience. Inside js and ts fences it runs the TypeScript language service, with the library declared as globals: completions, hover, signature help, go to definition (F12), references (Shift+F12), rename (F2), highlights and document symbols work across all fences of a document. Diagnostics report frontmatter, @CTL and JavaScript syntax errors exactly as the runtime compiles them, missing relative imports, and type errors inside ts fences. In blockquotes, @ completes shared variables, functions, library functions and markers; @validate[ completes declared types; @CTL( completes its settings. Names an agent saves (save to @invoice, -> @result) are declared for later fences. Type splworkflow for a new workflow document. Ordinary Markdown keeps its normal behavior. SPL: Open as Notebook runs fences and blockquotes as notebook cells in one session. The language server only analyzes source and never calls models or tools. See the extension guide for features, shortcuts and limits. Or install from the repository:","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Run the tests","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#run-the-tests","text":"Tests are offline and never contact a model.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Workflow code","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html","text":"Workflow code is native JavaScript running on Node.js, with the spl-lang library already in scope. This page covers what is specific to workflows: fences, the shared scope, imports, the library, types, the Markdown builder, inputs, logging, tools and errors.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Fences","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#fences","text":"A fence whose language is js, javascript or mjs runs as JavaScript. A ts, typescript or mts fence runs the same way after its type annotations are erased.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"One shared scope","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#one-shared-scope","text":"All fences and blockquotes of a document share one top-level scope.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Await model calls and tools","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#await-model-calls-and-tools","text":"ask, think, agent, human, codegen, restrict and the tools return promises. Use await whenever you need the result or the effect to have happened. A call without await keeps running in the background: its result may arrive after the document has ended, and its failure cannot be caught by the workflow and crashes the process. With --trace or --pin, recorded calls run one at a time in call order, so Promise.all stays replayable. Fences run as ordinary JavaScript on the main thread. A long synchronous loop blocks log rendering, timers and Ctrl+C handling until it yields; give long loops an await point, or move heavy work to a worker thread or child process.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Imports","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#imports","text":"Imports work as in any ES module, with one rule: specifiers resolve against the working directory (--cwd, by default the folder where spl runs), not the folder of the workflow file. Import Resolves to --- --- node:fs , node:path , node:child_process , ... The whole Node.js standard library ./helper.js , ../lib/util.mjs , ./legacy.cjs Your own modules, relative to the working directory ./helper.ts TypeScript modules with erasable types ./data.json with with { type: 'json' } JSON modules lodash , @scope/pkg Packages in the working directory's node_modules await import(specifier) Any of the above, loaded on demand Imported names join the shared scope, so later fences and blockquote agents can use them. To load a module that sits beside the workflow regardless of the working directory, build the path from import.meta: await import(new URL('./helper.js', import.meta.url).href). A helper module is ordinary JavaScript. It imports the library explicitly, and its calls take part in the running workflow: same model, restrictions, guidance, logs and trace. spl-lang and spl-lang/markdown always resolve to the running copy of the package, in fences and in helper modules, on the CLI and in VS Code notebooks. Helpers therefore need no local install and share the workflow's runtime and Failure class. Node.js loads it by its usual rules; to mark a helper as an ES module without relying on syntax detection, name it .mjs or set \"type\": \"module\" in the nearest package.json. --check verifies that static relative import declarations point at existing files; dynamic import('./x.js') calls are not checked. Modules are loaded natively by Node.js: they are cached for the lifetime of the process and their source is not recorded in traces or pins, so a replay imports them from disk again. imports/run.md is a runnable example.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["node:fs","node:path","node:child_process","./helper.js","../lib/util.mjs","./legacy.cjs","./helper.ts","./data.json","with { type: 'json' }","lodash","@scope/pkg","await import(specifier)"]},{"title":"The library","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#the-library","text":"Every fence can use these names without importing them. Outside a workflow, import them from spl-lang. API reference lists every signature and option. Group Names --- --- Model calls ask think agent People human Guidance and restrictions enforce forbid restrict Control success fail assert Failure Exact tools readFile writeFile webFetch webSearch shell Code generation codegen Inputs and logging args env log Types str int float bool char nil any object fn list union nullable enumOf struct typedef jsonSchema validate t Markdown markdown Model calls and guidance are described in Natural language & models; restrictions, failures and human input in Guards & failures.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":[]},{"title":"Types","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#types","text":"A type is a JavaScript value that describes data. It validates values, turns JSON into JavaScript objects and renders the JSON Schema a model receives. Pass one as schema to a model call, name it in a blockquote with @validate[Type], or use it directly. Builder Accepts --- --- str Strings int Safe integers ( Number.isSafeInteger ) float Finite numbers, including integers bool true or false char Strings of exactly one Unicode character nil null any Any JSON value object Any plain object, without a declared shape fn Functions; cannot be described as JSON list(T) Arrays whose items satisfy T union(A, B, ...) Values satisfying any member nullable(T) T or null ; a nullable field must still be present enumOf('a', 'b') One of the listed strings struct(name, fields) Exact records: every field required, extra fields rejected typedef(name, type, annotations?) The same values as type , under a name jsonSchema(schema, name?) Values valid against raw JSON Schema, constraints enforced Every type has the same methods: Method Result --- --- Type.parse(value) The validated value, or a validation_failed failure such as $.lines[0].quantity: expected int, got 1.5 Type.parseJson(text) Like parse , from strict JSON text; malformed text is invalid_format Type.is(value) true or false , without throwing Type.schema() JSON Schema, with annotations in description validate(value, Type) Same as Type.parse(value) ; with raw JSON Schema it checks and returns the value Parsing never coerces, fills defaults or drops fields: int.parse('1') fails, and a missing nullable field is an error. parse returns a copy for structs and lists. Error paths start at $ for Type.parse. When an agent saves a variable through a schema, the variable name is the root instead: invoice.tax: expected Money, got 3312.5. A type named with typedef appears under its own name, as Money does here.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["str","int","float","bool","char","nil","any","object","fn","list(T)","union(A, B, ...)","nullable(T)","enumOf('a', 'b')","struct(name, fields)","typedef(name, type, annotations?)","jsonSchema(schema, name?)","Type.parse(value)","Type.parseJson(text)","Type.is(value)","Type.schema()","validate(value, Type)"]},{"title":"Annotations are advice","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#annotations-are-advice","text":".annotate({...}), .describe(text) and the third argument of typedef attach arbitrary metadata for the model. desc or description becomes the schema description; every other key is appended as Model advice (not validation constraints): {\"minimum\":0}. Annotations never reject a value: Money.parse(-1) succeeds. Check business rules with assert, or use jsonSchema({ type: 'integer', minimum: 0 }) when a constraint must be enforced. Types are immutable: annotate, describe, as, nullable(), list() and or(other) return new types. struct('Point').field('x', float, { desc: 'Horizontal' }).field('y', float) is the fluent form of a struct.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Classes","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#classes","text":".methods({...}) gives parsed objects methods; .class(Constructor) makes them instances of an existing class, using its static fromJSON(data) when present. For a class type, is requires an instance, parse accepts plain data or an existing instance, and Person.data is the plain-struct view with the same fields. Instances serialize like plain objects with JSON.stringify.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"TypeScript","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#typescript","text":"In a ts fence, Infer gives the JavaScript type a builder parses to. Model calls are typed by their options, so invoice below is known to have a numeric total. TYPES.md records the full contract of the type builder.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Markdown builder","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#markdown-builder","text":"markdown builds Markdown text without string concatenation. Every method returns the document, so calls chain; toString() returns the result. Method Output --- --- heading(text, level = 1) Heading with level 1 through 6 paragraph(text) Paragraph containing inline Markdown bulletList(items) Bullet list of strings; multiline items are indented orderedList(items, start = 1) Numbered list quote(text) Blockquote, including multiline text codeBlock(text, language = '') Fenced code block; the fence grows to fit backticks in the text table(headers, rows) GFM table; rows must match the header width thematicBreak() Horizontal rule raw(text) Markdown as written toString() Blocks joined with blank lines and a final newline; '' for an empty document Inline helpers return strings: markdown.bold(text), italic(text), code(text), link(label, url), image(alt, url) and escape(text). Block methods accept Markdown, so use escape for literal dynamic text. Link labels and image alt text are escaped automatically, and table cells escape |. Invalid input, such as a heading level of 7 or a multiline heading, raises assertion_failed. Other modules import the builder from spl-lang/markdown; new markdown.Document() is equivalent to markdown.document().","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["heading(text, level = 1)","paragraph(text)","bulletList(items)","orderedList(items, start = 1)","quote(text)","codeBlock(text, language = '')","table(headers, rows)","thematicBreak()","raw(text)","toString()"]},{"title":"Inputs: args and env","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#inputs-args-and-env","text":"args is the read-only list of --arg values. env is the read-only process environment. Missing entries are undefined. With --trace or --pin, the first use of env records the environment so that replay sees the same values; process.env is not recorded. Blockquote agents read @args and one environment variable at a time with @env.NAME.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Logging","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#logging","text":"log(...values) writes to the run log on stderr at info level; log.error(...) and log.debug(...) select other levels. Debug output appears only with --log-level debug, and --silent hides all logs. Objects and arrays are logged as indented JSON: See console formatting.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Exact tools","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#exact-tools","text":"Tools are ordinary async functions that never call a model. Paths are relative to the working directory, and every call is checked against active restrictions. Function Result --- --- await readFile(path) File contents as UTF-8 text await writeFile(path, content) Writes the file, creating parent folders, and returns a confirmation await webFetch(url) Response body as text (30 s timeout, up to 5 redirects) await webSearch(query) Search results as text await shell(command) Combined stdout/stderr; a nonzero exit raises tool_error (120 s timeout) shell runs through the platform shell (cmd.exe on Windows, /bin/sh elsewhere). Agents see the same tools under the names read_file, write_file, web_fetch, web_search and shell.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["await readFile(path)","await writeFile(path, content)","await webFetch(url)","await webSearch(query)","await shell(command)"]},{"title":"Errors","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#errors","text":"Library failures are Failure objects: error.statusCode (also error.status_code) and error.reason say what happened. Catch them with try/catch and rethrow anything else. Any other uncaught exception, such as a TypeError in your code or an error thrown by an imported module, ends the document as a runtime_error whose reason is Name: message and whose line is the document line that threw. success(value) ends the run by throwing a signal that is not an Error; a catch-all must rethrow it. See Guards & failures.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"What the runtime does not see","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#what-the-runtime-does-not-see","text":"The runtime records, replays and restricts library calls. Code that reaches the outside world directly is your own responsibility: Library ( readFile , shell , webFetch , agent , MCP) Node.js APIs ( node:fs , fetch , child_process ) --- --- --- Checked by restrict Yes No Recorded by --trace and --pin Yes No On replay Recorded result is returned Runs again Values from Date.now(), Math.random() or a direct file read can differ between a recording and its replay. If such a value reaches a prompt or a tool argument, the replayed request no longer matches the recording and the run stops with a mismatch. Use the library tools for anything a replay must reproduce. Helper modules are in the same position: they are imported from disk again on every replay, so a trace or a locked workflow that imports local helpers depends on those files staying as they were.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["restrict","--trace","--pin"]},{"title":"The library in Node.js programs","page":"Workflow code","href":"language.html#the-library-in-nodejs-programs","text":"Outside Markdown, the library is a regular package. Calls use a default runtime: provider keys from the environment or spl login, info-level logs on stderr (override with SPL_LOG_LEVEL), the process working directory and stdin for human. configure(options) is optional and replaces the default runtime. Options: model, provider, cwd, args, logLevel (silent, error, info, debug), logger, backend, timeoutMs, mcpServers, trace and human. Running whole documents from a program is described under Embedding.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Natural language & models","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html","text":"Blockquotes let you write a step in plain language; ask, think and agent call the model from code. This page covers both, and how to check what comes back.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Blockquotes","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#blockquotes","text":"A blockquote is an agent command, not text that is compiled into code. The model receives the instruction, a JSON overview of the shared bindings, the active guidance and restrictions, and a set of tools. It acts directly on the shared scope through tool calls. For a simple generation task, the comparable code is: Blockquotes have these tools in addition to configured MCP tools: Tool Purpose --- --- load_variable(name) Read a shared variable, a library value, or the script arguments with the name args ; a type returns its complete JSON Schema save_variable(name, value, schema?) Create or update a shared variable; schema is the name of a type or schema binding, or an inline JSON Schema call_function(name, args) Call a shared function or library function with positional JSON arguments and return its result get_env(name) Read one environment variable; missing variables return null ask_human(question) Ask a person and return the answer read_file , write_file , web_fetch , web_search , shell The exact runtime tools restrict(kind, value) Narrow restrictions for the rest of the command, including function and MCP calls set_guidance(kind, text) Add enforce / forbid guidance for this command and its nested model calls success(value) , fail(reason) End the whole document These shared-scope tools are specific to blockquotes. agent(...) calls in code have the runtime tools and MCP tools only; ask and think have no tools. The overview sent with every blockquote contains the current value of each top-level binding: data as JSON, functions as signatures such as function add(a, b), types as labels such as \u003ctype Review>. Keep secrets and very large values out of top-level variables, or read them inside a function. @name marks a binding or operation; it is a reference for the agent, not text substitution. Plain language works too. Marker Agent behavior --- --- @notes , @summarize Load or save the shared variable; call the shared function @validate[Schema] Inspect the type Schema , then pass its name to save_variable ; an invalid value stops the command @success , @fail End the document with a result or an explicit failure @env.NAME Read one environment variable with get_env @args Read the script arguments With @validate[Review], the runtime resolves Review, validates the JSON value and saves what Review.parse returns: plain objects for structs, instances for class types. An inline JSON Schema, or the name of a binding holding one, only validates. Name composite types first, for example const Reviews = typedef('Reviews', list(Review)), then write @validate[Reviews]: the tool resolves binding names, not expressions such as list(Review). A rejected value reports the path from the variable being saved, for example invoice.tax: expected Money, got 3312.5. save_variable refuses to replace a function, a type, an imported name or a library name, and call_function passes JSON values as they are. Each tool call executes immediately and returns its real result to the same agent session. Any tool error stops the command at once, keeping its own status code; the model cannot retry, and completed actions are not repeated. A prose reply does not create variables, so follow important steps with a check:","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["load_variable(name)","save_variable(name, value, schema?)","call_function(name, args)","get_env(name)","ask_human(question)","read_file","write_file","web_fetch","web_search","shell","restrict(kind, value)","set_guidance(kind, text)","success(value)","fail(reason)","@notes","@summarize","@validate[Schema]","@success","@fail","@env.NAME","@args"]},{"title":"Controls","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#controls","text":"Put optional controls in one @CTL(...) block at the start of the blockquote: Setting Values Effect --- --- --- restrict A map with fs , exec , net or mcp keys; each value is a string or list of strings Narrow runtime permissions before the first tool call, like [ restrict ](Safety.md#restrictions) in code enforce , forbid A string or list of strings Add prompt guidance for this command and its nested model calls; not validation model A nonempty string Model for this blockquote; CLI --model still takes precedence reasoning 'low' , 'medium' or 'high' Reasoning effort of this blockquote's agent; defaults to 'medium' Controls are parsed and applied by the runtime, not interpreted by the model. Settings are literals, never JavaScript expressions: strings use single, double, backtick or triple quotes, r'...' is a raw string, and ${...} interpolation is rejected. Setting names and restriction kinds must be unique. Unknown settings, wrong value types and a misplaced or repeated @CTL are syntax errors, also with --check. Multiline settings and trailing commas are supported, and the marker is case-insensitive. All settings expire at the end of the blockquote. Restrictions intersect with those already active; guidance is inherited by functions the agent calls and by their model calls. model and reasoning select only the blockquote's agent. The former @Restrict, @Enforce, @Forbid and @Human markers are syntax errors: use @CTL, and request human input in plain language. --check validates syntax only; it cannot validate a blockquote's intent. --trace records the agent's decisions and tool results, and replay restores shared-variable changes without contacting the model or MCP. Run only trusted documents. Human input prompts in the shell and returns the answer to the same agent session; repeat --human \u003canswer> to answer questions in order without prompting. See Human in the loop.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["restrict","enforce","forbid","model","reasoning"]},{"title":"Code generation","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#code-generation","text":"@CODEGEN generates reusable JavaScript functions instead of running an agent command. In code, await codegen(description) does the same and returns the function. The marker must start the blockquote, after optional @CTL(...) settings, and takes exactly one nonempty literal string. codegen(description, { model, reasoning }) accepts the same two controls. The description syntax is checked by --check; generation happens only when running. The model receives the description, the shared bindings overview, the library function names and active guidance, with no tools. Its response must contain only function declarations (function name(...) or async function name(...)): the requested function first, then any helpers. One enclosing code fence is unwrapped; imports, exports and other top-level statements are rejected with invalid_format. The functions are defined in the shared scope without being called, can use the library and shared bindings, and stay available to later fences, blockquotes and interactive cells. With --trace, generated code is saved beside the workflow as \u003cworkflow>.\u003cfunction>.js, such as flow.double.js. Each file starts with // @CODEGEN sha256:\u003c64 hex digits>, the SHA-256 hash of the description. If the friendly file name is taken, the hash is added to the name rather than overwriting the file. Every new traced run looks for a sibling \u003cworkflow>.*.js file whose first line matches the description and uses it without a model call. A matching file that is not valid generated code fails; it is not silently regenerated. These files are editable JavaScript: review generated code before trusting it. The hash identifies the description, not the code, model or bindings. To regenerate, change the description or remove the cached file, then choose a new trace file. Replaying the same trace uses its recorded code, not the cache files. Without --trace, no cache is read or written. --pin embeds the generated code in the locked workflow, which then replays without the cache files.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Generation: ask and think","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#generation-ask-and-think","text":"await ask(prompt) returns the model's answer. think is the same with medium reasoning by default. Neither has tools: they cannot read files, browse or run commands. Each call starts a fresh conversation. Pass context explicitly, usually through a template literal. The tagged-template form takes no options; interpolated objects are rendered as JSON. Model responses stream into the interactive CLI view and notebook cell outputs while the model is working, including available reasoning by default. hide: true suppresses reasoning, not responses. These are previews: JSON parsing, schema validation and repairs use the completed response, never partial chunks. Traces retain complete responses rather than streaming chunks. Embedded integrations can use LoggerOptions.streamSink for request-scoped ModelStreamEvent updates. Backend callers can supply LlmRequest.onStream. Updates identify a response or reasoning channel and a message ID: append chunks with mode: 'append', replace content with mode: 'replace'. LlmResponse.text is always the authoritative return value.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Agents","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#agents","text":"await agent(prompt) lets the model act with the runtime tools (read_file, write_file, web_fetch, web_search, shell) and any configured MCP tools. Actions are checked against active restrictions. Agents default to medium reasoning and never use repairs, so a failed check never repeats their actions.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Options","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#options","text":"All three calls take an options object as the second argument. Values are validated before the call; an unknown name or invalid value is a runtime_error and no model is contacted. Option Values Effect --- --- --- len 'word' 'sentence' 'paragraph' 'long' Length hint (guidance only) fmt see [Output formats](#output-formats) Parse and check the answer schema A [type](Language.md#types) or JSON Schema draft-07 Validate the answer; implies JSON repairs 0 to 5 Extra attempts after a format or schema error; not for agent reasoning 'low' 'medium' 'high' Reasoning effort, if the model supports it hide true or false (default) Suppress reasoning display when true ; responses remain visible model Model ID Per-call model tokens Positive safe integer Maximum generated tokens per model turn, including reasoning seed Integer 0..2147483647 ask only: request repeatable sampling where supported temperature Number 0..2 ask only: control sampling variability topP Number 0..1 ask only: control nucleus sampling ( top_p is accepted as an alias) tokens is a hard generation budget, not a length hint or a separate thinking budget. The same limit applies independently to every repair attempt and agent tool-continuation turn. Omitting it keeps provider defaults. It maps to OpenAI max_output_tokens (or max_completion_tokens for seeded calls), Anthropic max_tokens, and Google maxOutputTokens. Legacy Anthropic manual thinking requires tokens to exceed its reasoning budget (low: 1024, medium: 4096, high: 16384); conflicting settings fail rather than silently enlarging the budget. If the model hits its token limit, the call fails with llm_error, not a successful partial answer, and repairs do not retry the truncated turn. Explicit budgets are part of the recorded request, so changing a budget cannot silently reuse an old answer during replay. Model precedence: CLI --model, then the per-call model, then frontmatter model, then the provider default. OpenAI, Anthropic and Google are supported through their official clients; use bare model IDs or provider/model. See Models and authentication.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["len","fmt","schema","repairs","reasoning","hide","model","tokens","seed","temperature","topP"]},{"title":"Randomness controls","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#randomness-controls","text":"Set sampling options directly on ask: Omitted sampling controls use provider/model defaults; no seed, temperature or topP is invented. The same values are reused for every repair attempt. think, agent and blockquotes do not accept sampling options. ask has no implicit reasoning effort. An explicit reasoning always takes precedence. Models that cannot disable thinking use the lowest supported thinking setting with a compatibility warning. Provider limitations are diagnosed with warnings; the runtime keeps supported controls without changing the model, and preserves explicitly requested reasoning: The response's effective.randomness records the controls actually retained by the provider adapter; null means the control was omitted or unsupported and provider defaults apply. Traces and pinned workflows include requested sampling controls in the request identity. Even extreme settings cannot guarantee identical outputs from hosted models. Use --trace or --pin for deterministic replay.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Output formats","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#output-formats","text":"fmt Accepts JavaScript result --- --- --- 'text' (default) Trimmed text, no parsing string 'integer' JSON number without a fraction number 'float' Any finite JSON number number 'boolean' JSON true or false boolean 'array' JSON array Array 'object' JSON object (not an array) plain object 'json' Any JSON value any JSON value Parsing is strict: prose around JSON and Markdown code fences are rejected, and values are never coerced. A malformed or wrong-typed answer raises invalid_format.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["'text'","'integer'","'float'","'boolean'","'array'","'object'","'json'"]},{"title":"Schemas and repairs","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#schemas-and-repairs","text":"schema accepts a type or raw JSON Schema. Both reject invalid output without coercion, defaults or removal of extra fields. A mismatch raises validation_failed with the path of the offending field. With a type, the result is what Type.parse returns: plain objects for structs, instances for class types. Struct fields are exact and required, including nullable fields. Annotations are advice for the model only. Raw JSON Schema is validated strictly with Ajv and returns the JSON value, for when constraints such as minimum must be enforced: repairs: n allows up to n more attempts. Each sends the original prompt again, followed by the previous answer and the validation error. Only invalid_format and validation_failed are repaired; transport and tool errors are not. Put business rules in assert, after the call. schema and repairs need JSON output: combining them with fmt: 'text', or using repairs without fmt or schema, is a runtime_error.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Guidance: enforce and forbid","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#guidance-enforce-and-forbid","text":"enforce(text, body) and forbid(text, body) add instructions to every model call made while body runs, including calls in functions it invokes and across await. They return what body returns. text is a string or a list of strings; nested guidance combines. Guidance shapes prompts; it does not check the answer.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Guards & failures","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html","text":"Model output varies, so SPL makes checks explicit: restrict limits actions, assert and schemas check data, failures can be caught, and human hands decisions to a person.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Restrictions","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#restrictions","text":"await restrict(spec, body) limits what tools and agents may touch while body runs, and resolves to what body returns. Outside any restriction, tools and agents are allowed by default. A blocked action raises restriction_violation. Spec Allows --- --- restrict('./out', body) or restrict({ fs: './out' }, body) Files inside the folder only restrict({ net: 'api.example.com' }, body) Only these hosts ( *.example.com matches subdomains) restrict({ exec: 'npm,git' }, body) Only these executables restrict({ mcp: 'docs/search' }, body) Only these MCP servers ( docs , docs/* ) or tools ( docs/search ) Each value is a comma-separated string or a list of strings. One object can combine several kinds. Nested restrictions of the same kind intersect, so a scope can only narrow. The restriction follows the call: it applies to everything body does, including functions it calls and code after an await, and ends when body settles. What is checked: Models are told the active restrictions, and the runtime enforces them on every call. ask and think have no tools at all. Restrictions govern the library, not Node.js. readFile, writeFile, webFetch, webSearch, shell, agents and MCP tools are checked. Code that calls node:fs, fetch or child_process directly is not: inside restrict('./out', ...), fs.writeFileSync('/etc/x') still runs. Use the library tools wherever a restriction should apply. For a blockquote, use @CTL(restrict={fs: ['./src'], exec: ['git']}) at its start. The runtime applies these restrictions before the agent runs, including to functions it calls and MCP tools, then restores the previous scope. See controls.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["restrict('./out', body)","restrict({ fs: './out' }, body)","restrict({ net: 'api.example.com' }, body)","restrict({ exec: 'npm,git' }, body)","restrict({ mcp: 'docs/search' }, body)"]},{"title":"Assertions, fail and success","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#assertions-fail-and-success","text":"All three work by throwing, so the statements after fail or success do not run.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Failure payload","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#failure-payload","text":"Every failure has the same shape. Uncaught failures are printed to stdout and exit with code 1. line is the document line that caused the failure and command is that line, trimmed. The line is read from the stack trace: the innermost frame that lies in workflow code. A failure inside an imported module therefore points at the workflow line that called into it. When no workflow frame is available, as for an agent's own tool call, the first line of the running fence or blockquote is reported. status_code Cause --- --- invalid_format Answer is not valid JSON of the requested fmt ; generated code is not function declarations validation_failed A value does not match its type or schema assertion_failed An assert was false; invalid input to the Markdown builder explicit_failure fail was called restriction_violation An action was blocked by restrict tool_error Missing file, HTTP failure or nonzero shell exit llm_error Authentication, timeout, transport, token limit or model access problem runtime_error An uncaught JavaScript exception, an invalid option or argument, a missing human handler, a replay mismatch of a pinned workflow Syntax errors are different: they are found before anything runs, cannot be caught, and exit with code 2.","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["invalid_format","validation_failed","assertion_failed","explicit_failure","restriction_violation","tool_error","llm_error","runtime_error"]},{"title":"try / catch","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#try--catch","text":"Failures are Failure objects and can be caught like any exception. Property Value --- --- error.statusCode , error.status_code One of the codes above error.reason , error.message The explanation error.payload() The JSON payload error.line , error.command The workflow line that raised the failure and its source text; 0 and '' when the line is unknown Three rules keep error handling safe:","group":"Writing workflows","signatures":["error.statusCode","error.status_code","error.reason","error.message","error.payload()","error.line","error.command"]},{"title":"Human in the loop","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#human-in-the-loop","text":"await human(question) asks a person and resolves to their answer as a string. In normal CLI runs, the shell prints the question on stderr and reads one line from stdin, preserving whitespace except the line ending. Execution continues immediately, without a suspension or repeating completed tool and model calls. Use repeatable --human \u003canswer> flags to supply exact answers in question order and skip prompting. Remaining questions prompt normally, including with --silent; EOF without an answer fails the run. Blockquote agents use the ask_human tool when asked in plain language. There is no special human marker. The answer is returned to the same agent session, which may then make new model calls and save it to a shared variable. Embedding hosts pass a human(question, line) handler to run; without one, human fails with runtime_error. Plain Node.js programs that use the library read stdin by default, or call the human option given to configure.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"Resuming with a trace","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#resuming-with-a-trace","text":"The same --trace path automatically replays recorded decisions, tool results and answers, then continues interrupted work. --human answers only questions not yet recorded; other new questions read stdin. Completed runs replay the same result without prompts or repeating recorded effects. Choose another JSONL file to restart. The workspace, source, runtime and model/provider/MCP configuration must match. Arguments and accessed environment values are retained. External files are not restored: changes made between runs stay changed. An interrupted side effect with no recorded outcome requires manual recovery rather than an unsafe retry. See Traces and replay for corruption handling and privacy considerations.","group":"Writing workflows"},{"title":"CLI & runtime","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html","text":"spl runs one Markdown workflow, prints its result on stdout and logs on stderr, and reports the outcome through its exit code. Requires Node.js >=24.15.0.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Usage","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#usage","text":"Option Description --- --- --arg \u003cvalue> Script argument exposed as args[i] ; repeatable, also --arg=value --human \u003canswer> Answer a human question without prompting; repeatable in question order, also --human=answer --model \u003cid> Model for every call, overriding per-call and frontmatter models --provider \u003cname> openai , anthropic or google ; model IDs also accept provider/model --cwd \u003cdir> Base folder for imports, relative paths, restrictions, tools and agents (default: current) --check Validate frontmatter, fences, blockquote controls, relative imports and --mcp config without running --log-level \u003clevel> error , info (default) or debug --silent No execution logs; human prompts still appear on stderr --timeout \u003cs> Per model call (default 300) --mcp \u003cfile.json> MCP servers, see [MCP servers](#mcp-servers) --trace \u003cfile.jsonl> Append execution history, automatically resume or replay the same file; see [Traces and replay](#traces-and-replay) --pin Save a successful workflow with its recorded effects as [locked Markdown](#pinned-workflows) -v , --version / -h , --help Version / usage Each --arg is one string; use --arg=--value for values starting with a dash. Only .md workflows are executable. --check compiles every js and ts fence, parses @CTL and @CODEGEN, and verifies that static relative import declarations point at existing files under --cwd. Dynamic import('./x.js') calls and package names are not checked. It runs no code and calls no model, so it cannot find errors that only appear at run time, such as a misspelled variable. Normal runs handle human calls and blockquote ask_human questions directly. The shell prints each question on stderr and reads one line from stdin, preserving whitespace except the line ending. Repeat --human to supply answers in question order without prompting: Supplied answers are exact strings, including empty or multiline values; use --human=--value for answers starting with a dash. Once supplied answers are exhausted, remaining questions prompt normally, even with --silent. EOF without an answer is a script failure, not a suspension. With --trace, recorded answers replay without prompting or consuming supplied --human values; those values answer only new questions.","group":"Reference","signatures":["--arg \u003cvalue>","--human \u003canswer>","--model \u003cid>","--provider \u003cname>","--cwd \u003cdir>","--check","--log-level \u003clevel>","--silent","--timeout \u003cs>","--mcp \u003cfile.json>","--trace \u003cfile.jsonl>","--pin","-v","--version","-h","--help"]},{"title":"Output and exit codes","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#output-and-exit-codes","text":"stdout gets the success value: strings as-is, everything else as JSON. A workflow that ends without calling success prints nothing. An uncaught failure prints its payload instead. stderr gets logs, including every model prompt and response, plus changes to shared variables with --log-level debug. Use --silent when logs could expose sensitive data. Each event has one header, without repeated continuation badges, panels or timestamps: Icon Category Color --- --- --- -> Model input: ask, think, agent, blockquote or codegen Magenta \u003c- Model output or runtime action Cyan x Error Red ! Warning Yellow i Information Blue . Debug Gray ~ Variable state Yellow Generic embedding messages have a - message header. Explicit log(...) calls keep their [info|error|debug] log: prefix for embedding/notebook compatibility. State logs cover the shared top-level bindings: each event names the binding, its previous value (\u003cunbound> on initialization) and its new value. Bindings are compared after every top-level statement and agent action, so a change made inside a function or loop is reported when that statement finishes. Local variables, unchanged values and imported modules are not reported. During trace replay, reconstruction is silent; newly completed steps remain visible. Terminal stdin and stderr enable an isolated BetterTUI view on stderr, even when stdout is piped. Event headers are bold when terminal styling is enabled. An animated trailing loading character remains visible while the workflow runs, including while waiting for model output. It disappears on exit and is never included in the expanded transcript or stdout. Generated model prefaces (runtime/document preambles, guidance and initial blockquote context) and every object/array start as folded summaries; the requested prompt stays visible. Up/Down selects a payload in the viewport or scrolls text; Enter/Space toggles a visible selection; Page Up/Down scrolls; Home/End reaches the ends. Other folds and selection survive arrival/resize. The view initially follows new output. Scrolling away from the bottom pauses following, not execution; scrolling back to the bottom with arrows, Page Down or End resumes following. Expanded JSON uses two-space indentation and preserves original numeric/string tokens and duplicate keys. Primitives and malformed JSON remain readable without misleading fold controls. Model responses and available reasoning stream live in this view by default; hide: true suppresses reasoning. Partial JSON is shown as text until the authoritative response arrives, when normal formatting and folding apply. Streaming previews are separate from immutable log events, so the final transcript contains each complete response once. Redirected logs remain expanded and append-only. --silent also suppresses streaming previews. @CODEGEN responses appear as JavaScript code with line numbers, preserved indentation and syntax colors when terminal color is enabled. The same display handles incomplete streaming code and definitions reused from cache. Generated codegen instructions and binding context start folded, leaving the requested function description visible. Plain logs and the final transcript retain the source without line-number gutters. Normal finish restores input/cursor/screen state and leaves one complete expanded transcript on stderr; no inspection keypress delays exit. Renderer/native failures are diagnosed and select plain append-only output without changing workflow results. Non-terminal stdin/stderr, TERM=dumb, embedded sinks, silent, help/version, auth and check-only never acquire the UI. Before a human prompt, the interactive renderer exits and restores the terminal; subsequent logs remain append-only so only the shell reads the answer. Supplied --human answers do not stop the UI. If terminal-mode queries are unsupported or an alternate screen is already active, SPL diagnoses the limitation and uses append-only output without taking over that screen. Message, label and value controls are sanitized rather than executed as terminal instructions. Colors are enabled automatically when stderr is a terminal, and disabled for redirected output or TERM=dumb. Set NO_COLOR=1 to disable color, or FORCE_COLOR=1 to enable it when redirecting; FORCE_COLOR=0 disables color, and NO_COLOR takes precedence. stdout results and trace journals remain plain, machine-readable data. --silent suppresses execution logs; usage, syntax and trace-usage diagnostics still appear on stderr so command-line errors are not hidden. --log-level retains ordinary severity filtering; model transcripts appear at every non-silent level, while state events appear only at debug. Forced color never enables cursor controls or keyboard input. For example, log('review:', review) produces: Embedded callers can pass structured data directly with logger.info('review:', review). Custom logger sinks default to plain text; new Logger('info', sink, { color: true }) explicitly enables styling. Non-JSON values such as errors, bigints and circular objects use Node's inspect format instead of losing error details or failing JSON serialization. An additive eventSink option delivers detached readonly events instead of strings, never both. Neither library imports nor custom sinks load BetterTUI or start a process. Code Meaning --- --- 0 Success 1 Script failure 2 Syntax or usage error, including a failed --check and a trace that does not match 70 Internal or infrastructure error","group":"Reference","signatures":["->","\u003c-","x","!","i",".","~","0","1","2","70"]},{"title":"Traces and replay","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#traces-and-replay","text":"--trace is the only CLI persistence and replay option. A missing file starts a run. With an existing file, the workflow's JavaScript runs again from the top while every recorded effect returns its recorded result, which rebuilds variables, functions and loops; execution then continues live at the first unrecorded effect. Records are appended and flushed durably: existing bytes are never rewritten. A completed success or failure replays, prints the same result and leaves the trace unchanged. To restart, choose a different JSONL file. The version 2 JSONL journal records the effects of the library: model requests, raw responses and failures (including repairs, reasoning, sampling and token budgets), blockquote decisions, readFile/writeFile/webFetch/webSearch/shell outcomes, restrictions, human answers, generated @CODEGEN code, accessed environment values and the final result. Completed effects are not executed again: replay never contacts model providers or MCP, re-reads files through the tools, repeats writes, commands or network calls, or prompts for recorded answers. It does not recreate side effects if their files have been removed or changed. Only library effects are replayed. Plain JavaScript is executed again on every run. Anything your code does through Node.js APIs directly, such as node:fs, fetch, child_process, Date.now() or Math.random(), is neither recorded nor suppressed. If such a value changes and flows into a prompt or tool argument, the request no longer matches the journal and the run stops with trace effect history mismatch. Helper modules are part of that plain JavaScript. They are imported natively again on replay and their source is not recorded: an edited helper can change the replayed result, and a deleted one makes the replay fail. Source, runtime, working directory and model/provider/MCP configuration must match. Arguments are retained; omitting --arg reuses them, while explicitly different arguments fail. Requests must match exactly and in order; malformed, mismatched and unused history fail without falling back to live work. After an interruption, a recorded human question or tool-free generation request can continue. If a write, shell command or agent was interrupted between its durable intent and outcome, its side effects may already have occurred: SPL reports an unresolved effect instead of repeating it. Truncated final records likewise require manual recovery; SPL never truncates the log to guess. Only one process can write a trace at a time; a lock from a terminated process is reclaimed. For code generation, traced runs also save \u003cworkflow>.\u003cfunction>.js sibling files with a description hash on the first line. New traced runs reuse matching files without generation calls. Replay of an existing JSONL file uses its recorded code regardless of later cache edits or removal. Calls that run concurrently, such as Promise.all(items.map(item => ask(...))), are recorded one at a time in call order when a trace or pin is active, so they replay deterministically. Keep traces private. They include prompts, responses, human answers and tool results. Reading env captures the environment snapshot for deterministic reconstruction; a blockquote get_env records only the requested value. These may contain secrets. New files use owner-only permissions where supported. Trace logging is not a transaction system or a sandbox.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Pinned workflows","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#pinned-workflows","text":"--pin runs the workflow normally. Only after successful execution and backend cleanup does it create a sibling flow.locked.md; the original file is unchanged, and an existing locked file is never overwritten. --check --pin only validates and writes nothing. Failed or interrupted runs do not produce a locked workflow. The locked document retains the original Markdown and appends a versioned JSON execution journal inside a double blockquote (>>) with a spl-lock fence. It includes every returned model response (including repair attempts and reasoning), blockquote runtime decisions, tool outcomes, generated code and human answers. This block is data, never another instruction to a model. The runtime recognizes the journal automatically, including through the embedding run API. Locked workflows run on their own; ExecutionSession rejects them instead of mixing pinned inputs with retained state. The JavaScript runs again while recorded effects return their recorded results: no model/provider or MCP calls, no library file/network/shell operations, no variable-store access and no human prompts. The script arguments, CLI model override and working directory are retained. When env or a blockquote environment tool is used, the environment snapshot is retained as well. Replay checks source identity, execution options, request order and complete journal consumption; missing, malformed, mismatched or unused entries fail without falling back to live calls. As with traces, only library effects are pinned. Code that calls Node.js APIs directly runs again, and helper modules are imported from disk again. A locked workflow that imports local helpers is therefore not self-contained: it needs those files, an edited helper changes the result and a deleted one fails. Keep everything a locked workflow must reproduce in its fences. Pinning cannot be combined with --trace. Locked workflows also reject MCP configuration and supplied human answers. Explicit argument, model or working-directory overrides must match the pinned values. To change the workflow or its inputs, run the original file with --pin again after moving or removing the old locked file. Keep locked files private. Responses, human answers, file contents, initial variables and any accessed environment snapshot can contain secrets. Pinning is not a sandbox: the original run still performs all requested actions; locked replay does not repeat those actions or recreate their effects.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"MCP servers","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#mcp-servers","text":"--mcp takes a file in VS Code's mcp.json format. MCP tools are available only to agents. Use the server names in restrict({ mcp }), and --check to validate the file without starting servers. Only load configurations you trust.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Models and authentication","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#models-and-authentication","text":"Every model call uses the official OpenAI, Anthropic or Google client in a fresh conversation. See Getting started for API-key login and environment variables. A call without credentials, or a provider/model access error, fails with llm_error. Workflows without model calls, check-only operations and replay do not initialize providers or MCP. Model precedence: CLI --model, per-call model, frontmatter model, provider default. IDs may be bare (claude-sonnet-4-5, gemini-2.5-flash, gpt-4.1) or qualified (anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5). Recognizable bare IDs infer their provider; custom IDs can be qualified or used with --provider. Unknown models are passed unchanged to the selected provider and fail if unavailable; there is no fallback to another model. Provider selection uses explicit --provider, then the model's prefix/inferred provider, then SPL_PROVIDER, then the saved login default, then a single configured provider. An explicit provider conflicting with the model's provider is an error. Multiple configured providers without a selection are an error, not an arbitrary choice. Defaults are gpt-4.1 (OpenAI), claude-sonnet-4-5 (Anthropic), and gemini-2.5-flash (Google). Reasoning levels map to OpenAI reasoning effort, Anthropic thinking budgets/adaptive effort, and Google thinking budgets/levels. Unsupported recognized combinations warn and use the model default; effective provider, model and reasoning effort are retained in traces. Runtime tools execute sequentially, MCP results remain traceable, and timeout/cancellation never accepts a partial answer. Failed actions are not automatically retried. Live output remains non-reproducible; contain variance with schemas, assertions and traces.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Runtime limits","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#runtime-limits","text":"","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Embedding","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#embedding","text":"The package that provides the CLI is also the library. Besides the workflow functions, it exports what is needed to run whole documents from a program. parseMarkdown compiles every fence and throws SplSyntaxError (with line and col) for invalid documents. run resolves to { status: 'success', value, hasValue } or { status: 'failure', failure }; hasValue is false when the document ended without success. run option Meaning --- --- backend , logger Required: the model backend and the log destination cwd Working directory for imports, relative paths, restrictions and tools (default: process directory) args Script arguments (default [] , never process.argv ) model Overrides frontmatter and per-call models, like --model human (question, line) => string or a promise of one; answers human and ask_human file Path of the workflow document; names generated-code caches source , sourceVersion URI and version reported as source and source_version in failure payloads trace , pin An open Trace or Pin to record and replay effects store Embedding-only initial bindings and final snapshot; not available with a trace Embedded runs never read stdin themselves; provide a human handler when a workflow asks for input. parse(code, { language, model }) wraps plain JavaScript (or 'ts') as a one-fence workflow, parseInstruction(text) wraps one blockquote, and checkWorkflow(workflow, cwd) returns the missing-import problems that --check reports. Use Trace.open(file, { source: workflow.lines.join('\\n'), runtime: 'your-runtime-id', cwd, args, configuration }) and pass the result as trace. After execution and backend cleanup, call trace.finish(result); always call trace.close() in finally. The same file automatically resumes or replays. A custom backend implements call(request) and close(), which makes offline tests simple: request.prompt is the fully assembled prompt and request.kind is ask, think or agent; only agent requests may run tools. For blockquotes the request has naturalLanguage: true, the runtimeTools definitions and invokeAction({ name, arguments }) to perform a tool call. AiBackend accepts an optional provider alongside logger, cwd, timeoutMs and mcpServers. The CopilotBackend export is an alias of AiBackend kept for existing imports, not a Copilot connection.","group":"Reference","signatures":["backend","logger","cwd","args","model","human","file","source","sourceVersion","trace","pin","store"]},{"title":"Interactive sessions","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#interactive-sessions","text":"ExecutionSession keeps one shared scope across calls, for notebooks and REPLs. Each call runs one cell; unlike run, the value of a cell's final expression is returned. Calls must not overlap. A failure keeps the changes made before it; nothing is replayed. source and sourceVersion are remembered by the functions a cell defines and returned in failure payloads, so a host can locate an error in the cell that defined the failing function. Sessions do not accept store, trace or pin. The host owns backend cleanup and cancellation; the VS Code notebook runs each session in a separate process so that even an endless loop can be stopped.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"API reference","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html","text":"Everything a workflow can use on one page: the document format, every library global with its signature and options, the type builder, blockquote tools and controls, and failure codes.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Document format","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#document-format","text":"A workflow is a Markdown file that starts with YAML frontmatter. Frontmatter field Required Value --- --- --- extension Yes semantic-prompt-language version Yes 1 model No Default model ID for the document preamble No Text added to every model request Unknown fields are syntax errors. After the frontmatter, blocks run in document order: Block Treatment --- --- Fence js , javascript , mjs Runs as JavaScript Fence ts , typescript , mts Types are erased, then it runs as JavaScript Fence with ignore or no-run after the language Not run Fence spl Syntax error with a migration hint Any other fence, headings, prose Commentary Top-level blockquote Agent command; @CODEGEN generates functions instead Double blockquote with a spl-lock fence The journal of a [pinned workflow](CLI.md#pinned-workflows) Fences are compiled as ES-module code in strict mode: top-level await, import, import() and import.meta.url, .filename, .dirname are available; top-level return, export default and export { ... } are not. import.meta describes the workflow file, while imports and relative tool paths resolve against the working directory. Imported names are live, read-only bindings. All blocks share one top-level scope; see One shared scope.","group":"Reference","signatures":["extension","version","model","preamble","js","javascript","mjs","ts","typescript","mts","ignore","no-run","spl","spl-lock"]},{"title":"Model calls","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#model-calls","text":"Call Resolves to --- --- ask(prompt, options?) The answer; no tools think(prompt, options?) Like ask , with reasoning: 'medium' by default agent(prompt, options?) The answer after the model acted with runtime and MCP tools ask Text ${value} Tagged-template form of all three calls, without options A prompt that is not a string is converted to text: objects and arrays as JSON, types by name. Option Type Default Calls --- --- --- --- len 'word' , 'sentence' , 'paragraph' , 'long' none all fmt 'text' , 'integer' , 'float' , 'boolean' , 'array' , 'object' , 'json' 'text' , or 'json' with schema all schema A type or JSON Schema (object or boolean) none all repairs Integer 0 to 5 0 ask , think reasoning 'low' , 'medium' , 'high' none for ask , 'medium' otherwise all hide Boolean false all model Nonempty string document or provider default all tokens Positive safe integer provider default all seed Integer 0 to 2147483647 provider default ask temperature Number 0 to 2 provider default ask topP (alias top_p ) Number 0 to 1 provider default ask The result is a string for text output, the parsed JSON value for fmt, and the parsed value of the type for schema. In TypeScript the return type follows the options. Invalid options are a runtime_error raised before any model call. See Options.","group":"Reference","signatures":["ask(prompt, options?)","think(prompt, options?)","agent(prompt, options?)","ask`Text ${value}`","len","fmt","schema","repairs","reasoning","hide","model","tokens","seed","temperature","topP","top_p"]},{"title":"Guidance and restrictions","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#guidance-and-restrictions","text":"Call Effect --- --- enforce(text, body) Runs body with positive guidance added to every model call inside; returns its result forbid(text, body) Runs body with guidance about what to avoid; returns its result restrict(spec, body) Runs body with narrowed permissions; resolves to its result text is a nonempty string or a list of them. body is a function, usually an arrow function. spec is a folder, a list of folders, or an object: spec key Limits Values --- --- --- fs Files read and written by tools and agents Folders or files, relative to the working directory exec Executables run by shell Names without a path, such as git net Hosts reached by webFetch , webSearch and agents Host names; *.example.com matches subdomains mcp MCP tools offered to agents server , server/* or server/tool Each value is a comma-separated string or a list of strings. See Restrictions.","group":"Reference","signatures":["enforce(text, body)","forbid(text, body)","restrict(spec, body)","fs","exec","net","mcp"]},{"title":"Control, people and logging","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#control-people-and-logging","text":"Call Effect --- --- success(value?) Ends the document with a result ( null when omitted) fail(reason?) Raises explicit_failure ; the default reason is failed assert(condition, message?) Raises assertion_failed when condition is falsy human(question) Resolves to a person's answer as a string log(...values) , log.info(...) Writes to the run log at info level log.error(...values) , log.debug(...values) Writes at error or debug level Failure The class of failures: error instanceof Failure , statusCode , status_code , reason , payload()","group":"Reference","signatures":["success(value?)","fail(reason?)","assert(condition, message?)","human(question)","log(...values)","log.info(...)","log.error(...values)","log.debug(...values)","Failure"]},{"title":"Tools","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#tools","text":"Call Resolves to Agent tool name --- --- --- readFile(path) The file's UTF-8 text read_file writeFile(path, content) A confirmation; parent folders are created write_file webFetch(url) The body of an http(s) GET web_fetch webSearch(query) The top results as text web_search shell(command) Combined stdout and stderr shell Arguments are strings. Failures are tool_error; blocked calls are restriction_violation.","group":"Reference","signatures":["readFile(path)","writeFile(path, content)","webFetch(url)","webSearch(query)","shell(command)"]},{"title":"Inputs and code generation","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#inputs-and-code-generation","text":"Name Value --- --- args Read-only array of --arg values env Read-only environment variables; access is recorded for replay codegen(description, options?) Resolves to a generated function, also defined in the shared scope; options are model and reasoning","group":"Reference","signatures":["args","env","codegen(description, options?)"]},{"title":"Types","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#types","text":"Builder Kind JSON Schema --- --- --- str str { \"type\": \"string\" } int int { \"type\": \"integer\" } float float { \"type\": \"number\" } bool bool { \"type\": \"boolean\" } char char String with minLength and maxLength of 1 nil null { \"type\": \"null\" } any any {} object object { \"type\": \"object\" } fn fn None: describing it is a runtime_error list(T) list { \"type\": \"array\", \"items\": T } union(A, B, ...) union { \"anyOf\": [A, B, ...] } nullable(T) union { \"anyOf\": [T, { \"type\": \"null\" }] } enumOf('a', 'b') , enumOf(['a', 'b']) enum { \"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"a\", \"b\"] } struct(name, fields?) struct Object with every field in required and additionalProperties: false typedef(name, type, annotations?) Kind of type Schema of type plus annotations jsonSchema(schema, name?) schema The given schema Member of every type Result --- --- kind , name , annotations The kind above, the display name and the attached annotations annotate(annotations) A copy with the annotations merged in describe(text) Shorthand for annotate({ description: text }) as(name, annotations?) A named copy; typedef is the function form nullable() , list() , or(other) union(T, nil) , list(T) , union(T, other) parse(value) The validated value; throws validation_failed with a path such as $.lines[0].total (the variable name replaces $ when an agent saves with a schema) parseJson(text) parse of strict JSON text; throws invalid_format for malformed JSON is(value) Boolean schema() JSON Schema with annotations in description Member of a struct Result --- --- fields The field types by name field(name, type, annotations?) A copy with one more field methods({ ... }) A class type whose parsed values have these methods class(Constructor) A class type whose parsed values are instances; a static fromJSON(data) builds them when present data The plain-struct view of a class type validate(value, type) equals type.parse(value); validate(value, schema) checks raw JSON Schema and returns the value. t holds every builder under one name: t.str, t.list, t.struct, with t.enum, t.null and t.schema as aliases of enumOf, nil and jsonSchema. TypeScript code gets the parsed type with Infer\u003ctypeof Type>, imported from spl-lang. See Types and TYPES.md.","group":"Reference","signatures":["str","int","float","bool","char","nil","any","object","fn","list(T)","union(A, B, ...)","nullable(T)","enumOf('a', 'b')","enumOf(['a', 'b'])","struct(name, fields?)","typedef(name, type, annotations?)","jsonSchema(schema, name?)","kind","name","annotations","annotate(annotations)","describe(text)","as(name, annotations?)","nullable()","list()","or(other)","parse(value)","parseJson(text)","is(value)","schema()","fields","field(name, type, annotations?)","methods({ ... })","class(Constructor)","data"]},{"title":"Markdown builder","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#markdown-builder","text":"markdown.document() (or new markdown.Document()) has the chainable methods heading, paragraph, bulletList, orderedList, quote, codeBlock, table, thematicBreak, raw and toString. markdown.bold, italic, code, link, image and escape return inline strings. See Markdown builder.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Blockquote tools","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#blockquote-tools","text":"Tool Arguments Result --- --- --- load_variable name The value; a type's JSON Schema; the script arguments for args save_variable name , value , optional schema The saved value call_function name , args (list) The function's result get_env name The value, or null ask_human question The answer read_file , write_file , web_fetch , web_search , shell As the tools above As the tools above restrict kind , value Narrows permissions for the rest of the command set_guidance kind ( enforce or forbid ), text Adds guidance for the rest of the command success value Ends the document fail reason Ends the document with explicit_failure Names must be JavaScript identifiers that are not reserved words. load_variable refuses env; save_variable refuses to replace functions, types, imported names and library names.","group":"Reference","signatures":["load_variable","save_variable","call_function","get_env","ask_human","read_file","write_file","web_fetch","web_search","shell","restrict","set_guidance","success","fail"]},{"title":"Blockquote markers and controls","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#blockquote-markers-and-controls","text":"Marker Meaning --- --- @name A shared variable or function, or a library function such as @readFile @validate[Type] Validate the value being saved against a declared type or schema @success , @fail End the document @env.NAME One environment variable @args The script arguments @CTL(...) Runtime settings; must open the blockquote @CODEGEN 'description' Generate functions; must open the blockquote, after optional @CTL @CTL and @CODEGEN are case-insensitive and parsed by the runtime; the other markers are conventions the agent follows. @restrict, @enforce, @forbid and @human are rejected. Strings are literals. Escapes are \\n, \\t, \\r, \\0, \\b, \\f, \\v, \\xHH, \\uHHHH and \\u{H...}; any other escaped character stands for itself, and raw strings (r'...') have no escapes. Single- and double-quoted strings end on their line; backtick and triple-quoted strings may span lines. ${...} inside backticks is rejected. Each setting and each restriction kind may appear once.","group":"Reference","signatures":["@name","@validate[Type]","@success","@fail","@env.NAME","@args","@CTL(...)","@CODEGEN 'description'"]},{"title":"Failure codes","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#failure-codes","text":"status_code Raised by --- --- invalid_format fmt parsing, parseJson , invalid generated code validation_failed Types, raw JSON Schema, @validate assertion_failed assert , the Markdown builder explicit_failure fail , the agent's fail tool restriction_violation Tools, agents and MCP inside restrict tool_error readFile , writeFile , webFetch , webSearch , shell llm_error Providers: credentials, access, timeouts, token limits runtime_error Uncaught JavaScript exceptions, invalid options and arguments, misuse of the library See Failure payload for the JSON shape and exit codes.","group":"Reference","signatures":["invalid_format","validation_failed","assertion_failed","explicit_failure","restriction_violation","tool_error","llm_error","runtime_error"]},{"title":"Package entry points","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#package-entry-points","text":"Import Contents --- --- spl-lang The library globals, configure , the type classes and Infer , plus the embedding API: parseMarkdown , parse , parseInstruction , run , ExecutionSession , checkWorkflow , Logger , AiBackend , Trace , Pin , loadMcpConfig and their types spl-lang/markdown The Markdown builder: Document , document , bold , italic , code , link , image , escape spl-lang/globals Types only: the fence globals as ambient declarations Inside a workflow, in fences and in the helper modules they import, spl-lang and spl-lang/markdown always resolve to the running copy of the package: no local install is needed, and helpers share the runtime and the Failure class. To type-check your own helper modules as if they were fences, add the globals to a jsconfig.json or tsconfig.json:","group":"Reference","signatures":["spl-lang","spl-lang/markdown","spl-lang/globals"]},{"title":"Maintaining this page","page":"API reference","href":"reference.html#maintaining-this-page","text":"The globals listed here are the keys of library() in lang/src/library.ts, which lang/globals.d.ts and the VS Code catalog mirror. When the library changes, update this page with them and run npm run docs:build from the repository root. The documentation tests compile every js and ts example and keep embedded example files in sync.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Examples","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html","text":"Every example is a runnable document in lang/examples. Run them from the lang folder, for example spl examples/hi.md: relative paths and imports resolve against the working directory. Each example's header shows its arguments.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Getting started","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#getting-started","text":"Example Shows --- --- [hi.md](../lang/examples/hi.md) A struct schema with one repair, a blockquote saving a shared variable, the Markdown builder [basics.md](../lang/examples/basics.md) Agents calling JavaScript functions, checking a model's answer against exact code, @CTL(enforce=..., forbid=...) [is-friday.md](../lang/examples/is-friday.md) Branching on a boolean answer [env.md](../lang/examples/env.md) Reading env and args in code, @env.NAME in a blockquote [color-poem.md](../lang/examples/color-poem.md) Asking a person in plain language, or supplying --human blue --human apple","group":"Guides","signatures":[]},{"title":"No model required","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#no-model-required","text":"These run offline, without credentials. Example Shows --- --- [typed-data.md](../lang/examples/typed-data.md) Enums, aliases, exact structs, nullable fields, unions, classes with methods, validation and JSON Schema output [markdown.md](../lang/examples/markdown.md) The Markdown builder [deterministic/node.md](../lang/examples/deterministic/node.md) Plain JavaScript with Node.js built-ins and top-level await [deterministic/enumsStructs.md](../lang/examples/deterministic/enumsStructs.md) parse and is on enums and structs [deterministic/types.md](../lang/examples/deterministic/types.md) Named types, annotations and their JSON Schema [imports/run.md](../lang/examples/imports/run.md) Importing a local .js module and node:os","group":"Guides","signatures":[]},{"title":"Developer workflows","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#developer-workflows","text":"Example Shows --- --- [code-review.md](../lang/examples/code-review.md) A restricted review agent, typed findings and a run that fails CI on high-severity findings [release-notes.md](../lang/examples/release-notes.md) User-facing notes from git log prepended to CHANGELOG.md [triage-issues.md](../lang/examples/triage-issues.md) Enums and a named list type; labeling GitHub issues with gh after human approval [codegen.md](../lang/examples/codegen.md) Generating functions with @CODEGEN and codegen() , cached with --trace [repo.md](../lang/examples/repo.md) An agent auditing the repository, with plain code deciding the outcome","group":"Guides","signatures":[]},{"title":"Data and documents","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#data-and-documents","text":"Example Shows --- --- [extract-invoice.md](../lang/examples/extract-invoice.md) Struct schemas and a Money alias with integer-cent arithmetic [types.md](../lang/examples/types.md) Unions and a class type as model output [translate-docs.md](../lang/examples/translate-docs.md) Translations verified to keep links, code and headings [incident-report.md](../lang/examples/incident-report.md) Exact log statistics, model-written narrative [web-digest.md](../lang/examples/web-digest.md) Web research with restricted fetches and checked citations [randomness.md](../lang/examples/randomness.md) Sampling options on ask","group":"Guides","signatures":[]},{"title":"Agents, state and people","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#agents-state-and-people","text":"Example Shows --- --- [mcp.md](../lang/examples/mcp.md) An agent limited to one MCP tool [journal.md](../lang/examples/journal.md) Explicit JSON journal state with resumable trace runs [human-review.md](../lang/examples/human-review.md) Asking for review directly, with optional trace resumption","group":"Guides","signatures":[]},{"title":"Extract an invoice","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#extract-an-invoice","text":"The struct schema checks the shape of the answer; a reusable Money alias documents that amounts are integer cents. Annotations such as minimum: 0 are advice for the model, not constraints; plain arithmetic checks that the numbers add up.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Triage issues","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#triage-issues","text":"Exact gh calls fetch and label issues; the model only classifies, and nothing changes until a person approves.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Plain JavaScript","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#plain-javascript","text":"No model is involved here: the workflow imports Node.js built-ins, declares a function and ends with a JSON result.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Migrating from 1.x","page":"Migrating from 1.x","href":"migration.html","text":"SPL 2.0 removes the custom SPL language. Workflow code is now native JavaScript in js fences, and everything the language had as syntax is a function of the spl-lang library. This guide maps every 1.x construct to its replacement and lists the behavior that changed. Migration is manual: there is no compatibility mode and no converter. A spl fence is a syntax error that points here. Blockquotes, frontmatter, CLI options, traces, pins, MCP configuration and logging work as before; recorded traces and locked workflows must be recreated because their source changed.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Before and after","page":"Migrating from 1.x","href":"migration.html#before-and-after","text":"SPL 1.x: SPL 2.0:","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Documents and modules","page":"Migrating from 1.x","href":"migration.html#documents-and-modules","text":"SPL 1.x SPL 2.0 --- --- spl fence js fence, or ts with erasable types helpers.spl module, every top-level name exported helpers.js ES module with explicit export import { double } from './helpers.spl' , relative to the importing file import { double } from './helpers.js' , relative to the working directory import * as markdown from 'markdown' Nothing: markdown is a global ( spl-lang/markdown in modules) No access to the host platform import fs from 'node:fs' and any package in node_modules Top-level return ends the document success(value) ; top-level return is a syntax error \u003cworkflow>.\u003cfunction>.spl codegen cache with a # @CODEGEN sha256: header \u003cworkflow>.\u003cfunction>.js with a // @CODEGEN sha256: header; old caches are ignored","group":"Guides","signatures":["```spl","helpers.spl","import { double } from './helpers.spl'","import * as markdown from 'markdown'","return","\u003cworkflow>.\u003cfunction>.spl","# @CODEGEN sha256:"]},{"title":"Statements","page":"Migrating from 1.x","href":"migration.html#statements","text":"SPL 1.x SPL 2.0 --- --- let x = 1 const x = 1; or let x = 1; let x: int = 1 const x = 1; ( const x: number = 1; in a ts fence); validate with int.parse(x) def add(a, b): with return a + b function add(a, b) { return a + b; } lambda x: x * 2 x => x * 2 if / elif / else with colon blocks if (...) { } else if (...) { } else { } for item in items: for (const item of items) { } for i in range(3): for (let i = 0; i \u003c 3; i++) { } for key, value in items(d): for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(d)) { } while cond: , break , continue while (cond) { } , break , continue pass An empty block { } try: / except err: try { } catch (error) { } # comment // comment Block and if expressions ( let x = : ) A conditional expression c ? a : b , or a function","group":"Guides","signatures":["let x = 1","let x: int = 1","def add(a, b):","return a + b","lambda x: x * 2","if","elif","else","for item in items:","for i in range(3):","for key, value in items(d):","while cond:","break","continue","pass","try:","except err:","# comment","if","let x = :"]},{"title":"Values and expressions","page":"Migrating from 1.x","href":"migration.html#values-and-expressions","text":"SPL 1.x SPL 2.0 --- --- True , False , None true , false , null (absent values are undefined ) and , or , not && , \\ \\ , ! a == b on lists and dictionaries === compares identity; use isDeepStrictEqual from node:util a if cond else b cond ? a : b a // b Math.floor(a / b) x in xs , x not in xs xs.includes(x) , !xs.includes(x) 'Hello {name}' Hello ${name} ; braces in ordinary strings are literal 'Use {{name}}' , r'C:\\temp' 'Use {name}' , String.raw with a template literal '''multiline''' A template literal object: block, object() { key: value } , {} len(x) x.length xs.append(x) , xs[1:4] , xs[::-1] xs.push(x) , xs.slice(1, 4) , xs.toReversed() range(n) , sum(xs) , sorted(xs) Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i) , xs.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) , xs.toSorted() keys(d) , values(d) , items(d) Object.keys(d) , Object.values(d) , Object.entries(d) text.upper() , text.strip() , text.startswith(p) text.toUpperCase() , text.trim() , text.startsWith(p) ', '.join(xs) , text.splitlines() xs.join(', ') , text.split(/\\r?\\n/) str(x) , int(x) , float(x) , bool(x) String(x) , Number(x) , Number(x) , Boolean(x) json_parse(text) , json_stringify(value, 2) JSON.parse(text) , JSON.stringify(value, null, 2) type(x) , isinstance(x, T) typeof x , T.is(x)","group":"Guides","signatures":["True","False","None","and","or","not","a == b","a if cond else b","a // b","x in xs","x not in xs","'Hello {name}'","'Use {{name}}'","r'C:\\temp'","'''multiline'''","object:","object()","len(x)","xs.append(x)","xs[1:4]","xs[::-1]","range(n)","sum(xs)","sorted(xs)","keys(d)","values(d)","items(d)","text.upper()","text.strip()","text.startswith(p)","', '.join(xs)","text.splitlines()","str(x)","int(x)","float(x)","bool(x)","json_parse(text)","json_stringify(value, 2)","type(x)","isinstance(x, T)"]},{"title":"Library calls","page":"Migrating from 1.x","href":"migration.html#library-calls","text":"SPL 1.x SPL 2.0 --- --- ask 'Summarize {notes}' await ask( Summarize ${notes} ) ask(fmt='integer', repairs=1) 'Rate {x}' await ask( Rate ${x} , { fmt: 'integer', repairs: 1 }) think(reasoning='high') 'Plan' await think('Plan', { reasoning: 'high' }) agent 'Run the tests' await agent('Run the tests') Modifiers hide=True , top_p=0.1 Options hide: true , topP: 0.1 human 'Approve?' await human('Approve?') enforce 'Be brief': block enforce('Be brief', () => ...) forbid 'jokes': block forbid('jokes', () => ...) restrict './out': block await restrict('./out', () => ...) restrict fs './src', exec 'git': block await restrict({ fs: './src', exec: 'git' }, () => ...) success value , fail 'reason' success(value) , fail('reason') assert cond 'message' assert(cond, 'message') read_file(p) , write_file(p, text) await readFile(p) , await writeFile(p, text) web_fetch(url) , web_search(q) , shell(cmd) await webFetch(url) , await webSearch(q) , await shell(cmd) $args[0] , $env.NAME args[0] , env.NAME log(level='debug')(value) log.debug(value) except err: with err.status_code , err.reason catch (error) with error.statusCode or error.status_code , error.reason","group":"Guides","signatures":["ask 'Summarize {notes}'","ask(fmt='integer', repairs=1) 'Rate {x}'","think(reasoning='high') 'Plan'","agent 'Run the tests'","hide=True","top_p=0.1","human 'Approve?'","enforce 'Be brief':","forbid 'jokes':","restrict './out':","restrict fs './src', exec 'git':","success value","fail 'reason'","assert cond 'message'","read_file(p)","write_file(p, text)","web_fetch(url)","web_search(q)","shell(cmd)","$args[0]","$env.NAME","log(level='debug')(value)","except err:","err.status_code","err.reason"]},{"title":"Types","page":"Migrating from 1.x","href":"migration.html#types","text":"SPL 1.x SPL 2.0 --- --- enum Color = 'red' \\ 'green' const Color = enumOf('red', 'green'); struct Card: with term: str [desc='Name'] const Card = struct('Card', { term: str.annotate({ desc: 'Name' }) }); type Money = int [minimum=0] const Money = typedef('Money', int, { minimum: 0 }); list[T] , A \\ B , T \\ None list(T) , union(A, B) , nullable(T) fn(str, int) -> str fn : any callable, without a signature class Person: with methods, hydratable class struct('Person', { ... }).methods({ ... }) or .class(Person) struct(Person) Person.data validate(data, T) validate(data, T) or T.parse(data) object(person) projection Person.data.parse(JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(person))) ask(schema=Card) 'prompt' await ask('prompt', { schema: Card }) Annotated bindings, parameters and results Not enforced; call T.parse(value) where a check is needed","group":"Guides","signatures":["enum Color = 'red' | 'green'","struct Card:","term: str [desc='Name']","type Money = int [minimum=0]","list[T]","A | B","T | None","fn(str, int) -> str","class Person:","hydratable class","struct(Person)","validate(data, T)","object(person)","ask(schema=Card) 'prompt'"]},{"title":"Blockquotes","page":"Migrating from 1.x","href":"migration.html#blockquotes","text":"Blockquotes are unchanged: the same agent tools, @name markers, @validate[Type], @CTL(...) and @CODEGEN. Three details differ:","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Behavior differences","page":"Migrating from 1.x","href":"migration.html#behavior-differences","text":"","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Embedding API","page":"Migrating from 1.x","href":"migration.html#embedding-api","text":"SPL 1.x SPL 2.0 --- --- parse(source) for SPL source parse(code, { language }) for JavaScript or TypeScript checkProgram , loadModules checkWorkflow(workflow, cwd) ; imports are native encodeJson , decodeTypedJson , schemaForType , validateType , validateValue , testValue , project JSON.stringify , Type.parseJson , Type.schema , Type.parse , Type.is , Type.data run(program, { source }) naming codegen caches run(workflow, { file }) ; source still labels failure payloads Language only available inside workflows import { ask, struct, ... } from 'spl-lang' in any Node.js program parseMarkdown, run, ExecutionSession, Logger, AiBackend, Trace, Pin and the backend interfaces keep their shapes. See Embedding.","group":"Guides","signatures":["parse(source)","checkProgram","loadModules","encodeJson","decodeTypedJson","schemaForType","validateType","validateValue","testValue","project","run(program, { source })"]},{"title":"Editor","page":"Migrating from 1.x","href":"migration.html#editor","text":"The VS Code extension no longer ships an SPL grammar, .spl language, snippets for SPL statements or a Markdown preview highlighter. Fences get JavaScript and TypeScript language features from the TypeScript language service, and notebook code cells are JavaScript or TypeScript cells. See the extension guide.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Checklist","page":"Migrating from 1.x","href":"migration.html#checklist","text":"","group":"Guides"}];
